description:
Create a new workflow as a JSON graph of nodes and edges.

Call workflow_list first to check that the id you choose does not already exist.
Call tool_list to discover valid action_id values before authoring tool nodes.

## WORKFLOW TOP-LEVEL FIELDS

  id          – unique kebab-case identifier, e.g. "research-report"
  name        – human-readable display name
  description – what this workflow does (optional)
  version     – semver string, defaults to "1.0.0" (optional)
  nodes       – array of WorkflowNode objects
  edges       – array of directed connections between nodes

## NODE SHAPE (every node)

  {
    "id":       "unique-node-id",
    "type":     "workflow",
    "data":     { ... },              ← node-type-specific (see below)
    "position": {"x": 0, "y": 0}     ← layout hint; increment y by 150 per step
  }

## NODE TYPES AND THEIR data SHAPES

### 1. parameters  — workflow inputs; must be the root node (no inbound edges)

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "parameters",
    "node": {"label": "Parameters"},
    "workflowParameters": [
      {"name": "topic",  "type": "string",  "description": "Topic to research",                     "required": true},
      {"name": "mode",   "type": "string",  "description": "Processing mode", "required": true, "enum": ["fast", "thorough"]},
      {"name": "count",  "type": "number",  "description": "How many items to return",              "required": false},
      {"name": "enabled","type": "boolean", "description": "Enable extra processing",              "required": false}
    ]
  }

  workflowParameters fields:
    name        – parameter name, used as the $input.<name> reference key (required)
    type        – one of: string, number, integer, boolean, object, array (required)
    description – shown to the LLM and in the run dialog to guide the caller (required)
    required    – true if the caller must provide a value; false if optional (required)
    enum        – array of allowed string values; the runner throws if the actual value is not in this list.
                  Use for fixed vocabularies (e.g. ["new", "existing"]).
                  The run dialog renders an enum param as a dropdown instead of a text input.
                  Automatically appended to the description shown to the agent at runtime.
                  Only meaningful for string, number, and integer types.

  • Values passed to workflow_run as `input` are mapped by name to these parameters.
  • All parameter values become available downstream as $input.<name>.
  • Always include a parameters node when the workflow takes any inputs.

### 2. prompt  — ask the session agent to reason, write, or analyse; capture its reply

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "prompt",
    "instructions": "Analyse the following about \"$input.topic\":\n\n$ctx.search_results",
    "node": {
      "label": "Analyse Results",
      "parameters": {
        "output": "analysis"   ← stores the agent reply in ctx; omit if not needed
      }
    }
  }

  • instructions supports $input.<name> and $ctx.<name> template interpolation.
  • output makes the result accessible as $ctx.<key> in all downstream nodes.
  • Use prompt for open-ended reasoning, writing, analysis, synthesis, or planning.

### 3. structured  — ask the agent to return a validated JSON object matching a schema

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "structured",
    "instructions": "Analyse \"$input.text\" and list all delegations.",
    "outputSchema": {
      "type": "object",
      "properties": {
        "delegations": {
          "type": "array",
          "description": "Top-level delegations found in the text",
          "items": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
              "title":    {"type": "string", "description": "Delegation title"},
              "owner":    {"type": "string", "description": "Who is responsible"},
              "children": {
                "type": "array",
                "description": "Nested sub-delegations",
                "items": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "title": {"type": "string", "description": "Sub-delegation title"}
                  },
                  "required": ["title"]
                }
              }
            },
            "required": ["title", "owner"]
          }
        }
      },
      "required": ["delegations"]
    },
    "node": {
      "label": "Extract Delegations",
      "parameters": {"output": "delegations"}   ← stores parsed JSON in ctx
    }
  }

  • instructions supports $input.<name> and $ctx.<name> template interpolation.
  • outputSchema is a standard JSON Schema object describing the required output shape:
      – Root must be {"type": "object", "properties": { ... }}
      – Property types: string, number, boolean, array, object (nestable to any depth)
      – For arrays use "items": {"type": "..."} or {"type": "object", "properties": {...}}
      – Add "description" to every property — the agent uses these to fill values correctly
      – Mark mandatory fields with a "required" array at each object level
  • The agent is forced via tool-calling to return exactly this shape (retried up to 2×).
  • output stores the result as a JSON string in ctx; downstream prompt nodes can
    reference it with $ctx.<key>.
  • Use structured when the output shape matters: extracted entities, scored lists,
    typed contracts, classification results, or any data a later step will parse.
  • Prefer structured over prompt whenever downstream steps need typed fields, not prose.

### 4. tool  — call a registered tool deterministically

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "tool",
    "node": {
      "label":     "Web Search",
      "action_id": "web_search",       ← call tool_list to see valid IDs
      "parameters": {
        "query":  "$input.topic",      ← tool argument; $input.* and $ctx.* resolved
        "output": "search_results"     ← stores tool return value in ctx; NOT a tool arg
      }
    },
    "agentArgs": []                    ← param names the agent fills at runtime (usually [])
  }

  • Every key in parameters except "output" is passed as a tool argument.
  • $input.* and $ctx.* are resolved before the tool is called.
  • output stores the tool's return value in ctx under that key.

### 4. decide  — branch on an agent choice or a deterministic condition

  Agent mode — LLM picks a case based on the current context:

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "decide",
    "node": {
      "label": "Quality Check",
      "parameters": {
        "mode":    "agent",
        "cases":   [{"label": "approved"}, {"label": "needs_revision"}],
        "default": "needs_revision",   ← fallback when agent response matches nothing
        "output":  "decision"           ← stores the chosen label in ctx (optional)
      }
    }
  }

  Deterministic mode — evaluates an expression against when-conditions:

  "data": {
    "nodeType": "decide",
    "node": {
      "label": "Score Gate",
      "parameters": {
        "mode":  "deterministic",
        "input": "$ctx.score",          ← expression to evaluate
        "cases": [
          {"label": "high", "when": {"op": "gte", "value": 8}},
          {"label": "low",  "when": {"op": "lt",  "value": 4}}
        ],
        "default": "medium"
      }
    }
  }

  Supported ops: equals, not_equals, in, not_in, contains, not_contains,
                 matches, not_matches, gt, gte, lt, lte,
                 exists, not_exists, is_empty, is_not_empty

## EDGE RULES

  Regular sequential edge:
    {"id": "e1", "source": "node-a", "target": "node-b"}

  Decide node — three edge kinds, combinable:
    • No label (or label "result")  → fires after ANY decision (always-run)
    • label = "<case-label>"        → fires ONLY when that case was chosen
    • label = "else"                → fires when NO labeled case edge matched

  A result-edge target AND a case-edge target may both fire for the same decision.

  Example decide edges:
    {"id": "e-log",     "source": "check", "target": "audit-log"}                  ← always
    {"id": "e-approve", "source": "check", "target": "publish",  "label": "approved"}
    {"id": "e-revise",  "source": "check", "target": "revise",   "label": "needs_revision"}
    {"id": "e-else",    "source": "check", "target": "fallback", "label": "else"}

## VARIABLE REFERENCES

  $input.<name>   – workflow input parameter (from the parameters node)
  $ctx.<name>     – stored output of a previous node (set via node.parameters.output)
  $output.<name>  – alias for $ctx.<name>

  In instructions (template interpolation):
    "Summarise this: $ctx.search_results and relate it to $input.topic"

  In parameters values (resolved before tool call or decide evaluation):
    {"query": "$input.topic", "context": "$ctx.prior_result"}

## LOOPS

  Loops are NOT supported. The runner visits each node at most once; back-edges
  are silently skipped. For iterative tasks use a sequence of prompt nodes, a
  decide that branches to a later node, or call workflow_run multiple times.

## DESIGN GUIDELINES

  1. Start with a parameters node when the workflow takes inputs.
  2. Use prompt nodes for reasoning, writing, analysis, and synthesis.
  3. Use tool nodes for deterministic I/O: search, file read/write, bash, etc.
  4. Use decide (agent mode) for qualitative judgements; (deterministic) for
     numeric/boolean/string conditions on ctx values.
  5. Give every node a descriptive label — decide and prompt nodes read labels
     for context.
  6. Name output keys semantically ("draft_report", not "result1").
  7. Store all intermediate results you need downstream via output keys.

## COMPLETE EXAMPLE — research-report workflow

  {
    "id": "research-report",
    "name": "Research and Report",
    "description": "Search the web, analyse findings, write a structured report",
    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": "params", "type": "workflow",
        "data": {
          "nodeType": "parameters",
          "node": {"label": "Parameters"},
          "workflowParameters": [{"name": "topic", "type": "string", "description": "Topic to research", "required": true}]
        },
        "position": {"x": 0, "y": 0}
      },
      {
        "id": "search", "type": "workflow",
        "data": {
          "nodeType": "tool",
          "node": {
            "label": "Web Search",
            "action_id": "web_search",
            "parameters": {"query": "$input.topic", "output": "search_results"}
          },
          "agentArgs": []
        },
        "position": {"x": 0, "y": 150}
      },
      {
        "id": "analyse", "type": "workflow",
        "data": {
          "nodeType": "prompt",
          "instructions": "Analyse these search results about \"$input.topic\" and extract the key insights:\n\n$ctx.search_results",
          "node": {"label": "Analyse Results", "parameters": {"output": "analysis"}}
        },
        "position": {"x": 0, "y": 300}
      },
      {
        "id": "report", "type": "workflow",
        "data": {
          "nodeType": "prompt",
          "instructions": "Write a comprehensive, well-structured report on \"$input.topic\" based on this analysis:\n\n$ctx.analysis",
          "node": {"label": "Write Report", "parameters": {"output": "report"}}
        },
        "position": {"x": 0, "y": 450}
      }
    ],
    "edges": [
      {"id": "e1", "source": "params",   "target": "search"},
      {"id": "e2", "source": "search",   "target": "analyse"},
      {"id": "e3", "source": "analyse",  "target": "report"}
    ]
  }
